 "summary": "In serving, #986 changes the draft budget only at c16, and by little: verified rows -0.87% (44.61 -> 44.22 a step, boot spread 0.06-0.08), tokens -0.44%, wasted rows -2.3%, coverage at positions 1-4 down 0.002-0.007. At c8 and c1 it picks the same rows (c1 identical by construction: one request, at most 6 rows, every #986 term is zero). The regimes its screen priced are absent from the release suite (contexts <= 285 tokens; c1 <= 6 rows; c8 <= 48 rows) or rare (c16: about 8.5% of steps above 64 rows in a replay that reproduces the served change). Where it fires, the predicted saving was the screen's own latency model (a non-monotone TP1 dense table on zero tensors plus a hand-set 440 us/row MoE term above 64 rows), not a cost the tp4-ring engine grid shows: the grid has no context cost from 4K to 1M and prices 64 -> 96 rows at about what 1107's 0.55 slope already charges. A replay of the real 1107 and #986 modules over steps fitted to the served counters predicts |change| <= 0.16% at c16, <= 0.01% at c8 and 0 at c1 for #986 and for a knee re-calibrated to the grid, under every step-time model tried; the A/A floors are 0.43/0.39/0.35%. No correction of #986's terms can be confirmed in serving, and the context band, the one part serving would hit (production's 64-100K agent prompts), prices a cost that does not exist on tp4-ring.",
